    "description": "A roundup of the most-asked questions about 30/60/90 onboarding plans — what the windows mean, how role and seniority change the plan, who runs check-ins, the first-week checklist, and what a plan cannot promise.",
    "html": "<h2>Why a 30/60/90 plan at all</h2>\n<p>The format exists to make a new hire's first quarter legible. Three checkpoints — learn by 30, do by 60, own by 90 — turn \"get up to speed\" into something a manager and a new hire can both track. It is a planning aid, not a guarantee.</p>\n<h2>How role and seniority change the plan</h2>\n<p>The three windows stay fixed; the expectations inside them move with the person. A junior hire spends window one on fundamentals, while a manager is expected to own outcomes sooner. OnboardPlan role-tunes the plan to Junior, Mid, Senior, or Manager from a single input.</p>\n<h2>Check-ins and owners</h2>\n<p>Without named check-ins, a plan decays into a document. The usual cadence is a week-1 manager check-in, a week-4 manager-plus-buddy check-in, and a week-12 manager review. Naming an owner is what makes the calendar remind instead of the manager remember.</p>\n<h2>The first-week checklist</h2>\n<p>The first week is mostly logistics — laptop and system access, intro meetings, reading the handbook — and it shifts with work mode. Remote, onsite, and hybrid hires need different access and intro paths, so the checklist should adapt rather than be copied.</p>\n<h2>What a plan cannot promise</h2>\n<p>A plan clarifies expectations and gives managers a check-in framework. It does <strong>not</strong> promise retention or onboarding success. Those depend on the manager, the team, the role, and the individual. Frame any plan as a shared map, not a contract.</p>\n<h2>Further reading</h2>\n<ul><li>Society for Human Resource Management (SHRM): https://www.shrm.org/</li><li>Gallup workplace research: https://www.gallup.com/</li></ul>\n",
