    "description": "A 30/60/90 onboarding plan earns its place for new managers, scale-ups hiring repeat roles, remote teams, HR generalists standardizing process, and any function with a clear ramp. Here are the use cases.",
    "html": "<h2>New managers hiring their first reports</h2>\n<p>A first-time manager often has no onboarding playbook of their own. A 30/60/90 plan gives them a default structure — what to expect at each window and when to check in — so the new hire's ramp does not depend on the manager's memory.</p>\n<h2>Scale-ups repeating the same roles</h2>\n<p>When you hire the same role every few months, a reusable plan pays off. OnboardPlan saves history on Pro, so the second SDR or support engineer starts from a tuned plan, not a blank doc. Reuse is where the format saves the most time.</p>\n<h2>Remote and distributed teams</h2>\n<p>Remote onboarding has no hallway learning, so the first-week checklist and the check-in calendar matter more. The plan makes async expectations explicit and gives a remote manager fixed points to sync.</p>\n<h2>HR generalists standardizing onboarding</h2>\n<p>HR teams that want consistent expectations across managers can ship a role-tuned template per function. The plan becomes the shared language for \"ramped by when,\" reducing variance between managers.</p>\n<h2>Roles with a clear ramp</h2>\n<p>Sales, customer success, and support have observable early metrics, so the do/own windows map cleanly to real deliverables. The format fits any function, but it is sharpest where \"on track\" is measurable.</p>\n<h2>When it is less useful</h2>\n<p>For a one-time, highly unique role with no repeat hires, a full plan may be overkill — a checklist plus a few check-ins can suffice. The plan is a planning aid; use the lightest structure that makes expectations explicit.</p>\n<h2>Further reading</h2>\n<ul><li>Society for Human Resource Management (SHRM): https://www.shrm.org/</li><li>Gallup workplace research: https://www.gallup.com/</li></ul>\n",
