    "slug": "30-60-90-plan-vs-generic-checklist",
    "title": "30/60/90 plan vs a generic onboarding checklist",
    "datePublished": "2026-07-27",
    "dateModified": "2026-07-27",
    "description": "A generic onboarding checklist lists tasks; a 30/60/90 plan sequences them into learn/do/own windows with role-tuned goals and named check-ins. They solve different problems and work best together.",
    "html": "<h2>A checklist lists tasks; a plan sequences them</h2>\n<p>A <strong>generic onboarding checklist</strong> is a flat list: \"set up email, read the handbook, meet the team, complete training.\" It is a good memory aid and ensures nothing obvious is skipped. But it does not say <em>when</em> each item matters or <em>what good looks like</em> at day 30, 60, and 90.</p>\n<p>A <strong>30/60/90 plan</strong> puts those tasks into three windows and attaches a goal to each: learn by 30, do by 60, own by 90. The checklist answers \"did we cover it?\"; the plan answers \"are they on track?\"</p>\n<h2>Where the checklist wins</h2>\n<p>The checklist is unbeatable for first-week logistics — access, accounts, equipment, intro meetings. These are binary and role-independent, so a shared list is the right tool. OnboardPlan still emits a first-week checklist inside the plan for exactly this reason.</p>\n<h2>Where the plan wins</h2>\n<p>The plan wins on expectations and timing. A new hire and a manager both know what \"ramped\" means at each checkpoint, and the named check-ins create natural moments to course-correct. A flat checklist has no such calendar; gaps surface only at the end of probation.</p>\n<h2>They are complementary, not competitors</h2>\n<p>Use the checklist for logistics and the plan for expectations. OnboardPlan combines both: a role-tuned 30/60/90 structure with a first-week checklist attached. One without the other leaves a gap — a checklist with no trajectory, or a plan with no logistics.</p>\n<h2>What neither replaces</h2>\n<p>Neither a checklist nor a plan guarantees a new hire will be retained or successful. They are planning aids that make expectations explicit; the actual outcome depends on the manager, the team, the role, and the person.</p>\n<h2>Further reading</h2>\n<ul><li>Society for Human Resource Management (SHRM): https://www.shrm.org/</li><li>Harvard Business Review: https://hbr.org/</li></ul>\n",
