    "slug": "common-30-60-90-onboarding-mistakes",
    "title": "Common 30/60/90 onboarding plan mistakes",
    "datePublished": "2026-07-27",
    "dateModified": "2026-07-27",
    "description": "The most common 30/60/90 onboarding plan mistakes are vague goals, unnamed check-ins, one plan for every level, ignoring work mode, and writing the plan once and forgetting it. Here is how to avoid each.",
    "html": "<h2>Mistake 1 — goals too vague</h2>\n<p>\"Understand the product\" and \"get up to speed\" cannot be checked. A vague goal passes the plan review and fails the real test at day 30. Fix it by writing observable outcomes: \"can demo the core flow to a peer\" beats \"understands the product.\"</p>\n<h2>Mistake 2 — no named check-ins</h2>\n<p>A plan with goals but no check-ins assumes the manager will remember to look. They often do not. Assign a specific owner and week to each check-in — week 1 (manager), week 4 (manager + buddy), week 12 (manager) — so the calendar does the reminding.</p>\n<h2>Mistake 3 — one plan for every level</h2>\n<p>A junior and a manager should not see the same day-60 expectation. Applying one generic plan to all seniorities sets the wrong bar and wastes the senior hire's first weeks. Role-tune the plan to Junior, Mid, Senior, or Manager.</p>\n<h2>Mistake 4 — ignoring work mode</h2>\n<p>The first week looks different remote vs onsite. Copying an onsite checklist onto a remote hire leaves them waiting on access or missing intro meetings. Adjust the first-week checklist to Remote, Onsite, or Hybrid.</p>\n<h2>Mistake 5 — written once, never revisited</h2>\n<p>A plan written at offer time and opened at day 90 is a document, not a process. The point of the three windows is to catch gaps early. Revisit the plan at each check-in and edit it as the role's real needs appear.</p>\n<h2>What avoiding these mistakes does not do</h2>\n<p>Fixing these errors makes expectations explicit and check-ins real. It does <strong>not</strong> guarantee the new hire will be retained or succeed — that still depends on the manager, the team, the role, and the person. The plan is a planning aid, not a promise.</p>\n<h2>Further reading</h2>\n<ul><li>Society for Human Resource Management (SHRM): https://www.shrm.org/</li><li>Harvard Business Review: https://hbr.org/</li></ul>\n",
