    "slug": "how-to-build-30-60-90-onboarding-plan",
    "title": "How to build a 30/60/90 day onboarding plan by role",
    "datePublished": "2026-07-27",
    "dateModified": "2026-07-27",
    "description": "A four-step framework to build a role-based 30/60/90 onboarding plan — name the role, set window goals, assign check-ins and owners, and add a first-week checklist — then hand it to the manager to adjust.",
    "html": "<h2>Step 1 — name the role, level, and work mode</h2>\n<p>Start with the specifics that change the plan: the <strong>role</strong> (e.g. \"Sales Development Rep\"), the <strong>seniority</strong> (Junior, Mid, Senior, Manager), and the <strong>work mode</strong> (Remote, Onsite, Hybrid). A junior hire needs more scaffolding in window one; a manager needs ownership expectations sooner. Remote and onsite roles differ mainly in the first-week checklist — access, intro meetings, and where the handbook lives.</p>\n<h2>Step 2 — set goals for each window</h2>\n<p>For each of the three windows, write concrete goals and the expected outcome:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Days 0–30 (Learn)</strong>: training complete, one peer shadow, first small owned task shipped.</li><li><strong>Days 31–60 (Do)</strong>: one recurring deliverable owned end to end, one internal review led.</li><li><strong>Days 61–90 (Own)</strong>: an initiative driven with light oversight, a written playbook shared.</li></ul>\n<p>Keep each goal observable. \"Understand the product\" is weaker than \"can demo the core flow to a peer.\"</p>\n<h2>Step 3 — assign check-ins and owners</h2>\n<p>Add 2–3 check-ins with a suggested owner:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Week 1</strong> — manager: access, first impressions, unblock.</li><li><strong>Week 4</strong> — manager + buddy: is the fundamentals window on track?</li><li><strong>Week 12</strong> — manager: can they own outcomes yet?</li></ul>\n<p>Naming an owner turns a wish into a calendar entry. OnboardPlan returns these check-ins from the role input so the manager is not starting from a blank page.</p>\n<h2>Step 4 — add a first-week checklist, then adjust</h2>\n<p>List the first-week essentials: laptop and system access, intro meetings, and reading the handbook. Then hand the draft to the hiring manager to edit — real teams have quirks a template cannot know. OnboardPlan produces the draft; the manager owns the final plan.</p>\n<h2>An honest limit</h2>\n<p>A plan structures expectations; it does not guarantee a new hire ramps fast or stays. Use it as a shared map for check-ins, and adjust it as you learn what the role actually needs.</p>\n<h2>Further reading</h2>\n<ul><li>Society for Human Resource Management (SHRM): https://www.shrm.org/</li><li>Gallup workplace research: https://www.gallup.com/</li></ul>\n",
