    "description": "Teams build 30/60/90 onboarding plans with spreadsheets, LMS courses, HRIS workflows, or free templates. Each trades effort, structure, and reuse differently — here is how they compare as OnboardPlan alternatives.",
    "html": "<h2>Spreadsheets</h2>\n<p>A shared spreadsheet is the most common starting point. It is free, flexible, and everyone knows how to use it. The cost is that every plan is hand-built, structure drifts between managers, and history is hard to reuse when the same role is hired again. Good for one-off plans; weak at scale.</p>\n<h2>LMS courses</h2>\n<p>A learning management system delivers training content but usually stops at \"completed the course.\" It is strong on compliance and knowledge delivery, weaker on the do/own windows and on role-tuned expectations. An LMS answers \"did they train?\" not \"are they on track by day 60?\"</p>\n<h2>HRIS workflows</h2>\n<p>HR software handles the records, documents, and approvals around onboarding. It is the system of record, not usually a planning canvas. Many HRIS tools include onboarding checklists, but those tend to be generic task lists rather than role-tuned 30/60/90 plans with named check-ins.</p>\n<h2>Free templates</h2>\n<p>A downloaded 30/60/90 template is a fast, free skeleton. The limit is that it is static: you still fill in every role by hand, and it does not adapt to seniority or work mode. Useful as a reference; tedious as a repeatable process.</p>\n<h2>Where OnboardPlan fits</h2>\n<p>OnboardPlan sits between a blank template and heavy HR tooling: you enter role, seniority, and work mode, and it returns a role-tuned 30/60/90 plan with check-ins, owners, and a first-week checklist — unlimited and saved on Pro. It is a planning aid, not a replacement for your HRIS or LMS.</p>\n<h2>What none of these guarantee</h2>\n<p>No tool — spreadsheet, LMS, HRIS, template, or OnboardPlan — guarantees a new hire will be retained or succeed. The plan makes expectations explicit; the outcome still depends on the manager, the team, the role, and the person.</p>\n<h2>Further reading</h2>\n<ul><li>Society for Human Resource Management (SHRM): https://www.shrm.org/</li></ul>\n",
