    "description": "A 30/60/90 day onboarding plan splits a new hire's first quarter into three windows — learn by day 30, do by day 60, own by day 90 — so expectations, goals, and check-ins are explicit per role.",
    "html": "<h2>What a 30/60/90 day onboarding plan is</h2>\n<p>A <strong>30/60/90 day onboarding plan</strong> is a structured outline of what a new hire should learn, do, and own during their first three months. The quarter is split into three windows:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Days 0–30 — Learn</strong>: training, tooling, context, and the first small owned task.</li><li><strong>Days 31–60 — Do</strong>: owning a recurring deliverable end to end and contributing without constant hand-holding.</li><li><strong>Days 61–90 — Own</strong>: driving an initiative with light oversight and documenting how the work is done.</li></ul>\n<p>The point is to make the first 90 days legible — to the manager, the new hire, and the team — instead of leaving ramp-up to chance.</p>\n<h2>Why the three windows matter</h2>\n<p>A single \"get up to speed\" goal is too vague to manage. Splitting it into three checkpoints gives everyone a shared calendar:</p>\n<ul><li>The <strong>30-day</strong> mark answers \"have they got the basics?\"</li><li>The <strong>60-day</strong> mark answers \"can they deliver on their own?\"</li><li>The <strong>90-day</strong> mark answers \"can they own outcomes?\"</li></ul>\n<p>Each window is a natural moment for a check-in, so small gaps get caught early rather than at the end of probation.</p>\n<h2>What goes in each window</h2>\n<p>For a given role, a plan names concrete goals and expected outcomes per window, plus the owners of each check-in. OnboardPlan generates this by role, seniority, and work mode, then returns 2–3 check-ins with suggested owners and a first-week checklist. The output is a plan you can share and adjust — not a document that sits unread.</p>\n<h2>Who it is for</h2>\n<p>The format fits any function with a defined ramp: sales, engineering, customer success, marketing, ops, and support. The plan is <strong>role-tuned</strong>, so a junior hire and a manager see different expectations for the same window. Remote, onsite, and hybrid work modes change the first-week checklist, not the structure.</p>\n<h2>What a plan cannot do</h2>\n<p>A plan is a planning aid. It clarifies expectations and gives managers a framework for check-ins, but it does <strong>not</strong> guarantee that a new hire will be retained or that onboarding will succeed. Outcomes depend on the manager, the team, the role, and the person. Treat the plan as a shared map, not a promise.</p>\n<h2>Further reading</h2>\n<ul><li>Society for Human Resource Management (SHRM): https://www.shrm.org/</li><li>Gallup workplace research: https://www.gallup.com/</li><li>Harvard Business Review: https://hbr.org/</li></ul>\n",
